## System Design

The LinkedIn Automation Platform is designed as a secure, event-driven backend system that manages OAuth authentication, persona storage, content scheduling, and reliable posting workflows. The architecture separates user-facing operations from background automation tasks to ensure responsiveness, reliability, and scalability.

### Core Components

API Service

Responsible for handling client requests including LinkedIn OAuth connection, persona management, draft approvals, scheduling operations, and configuration retrieval. The API layer performs validation, authentication, authorization, and persistence of metadata.

OAuth / LinkedIn Integration Module

Manages LinkedIn OAuth flow, token exchange, refresh handling, and API communication with LinkedIn services. Tokens are never exposed to clients and are securely stored after encryption.

Database (PostgreSQL)

Stores relational metadata including users, LinkedIn accounts, personas, drafts, schedules, and posting logs. PostgreSQL is selected due to strong consistency guarantees, transactional safety, indexing flexibility, and suitability for relational workflows.

Job Queue

Decouples scheduling and posting operations from synchronous API requests. Posting tasks, retries, and delayed executions are handled asynchronously through queued jobs.

Worker Services

Consume jobs from the queue and perform automated actions such as scheduled posting, retry processing, rate limit handling, and audit logging. Workers ensure fault tolerance and controlled execution.

Configuration / Prompt Store

Stores prompt versions or configuration packs provided by the GenAI team. Versioning enables controlled rollouts, reproducibility, and rollback capabilities.

